      and creates the content/ directory. (PR #34, commit 6cdc23c)
2026-10-24:
    - Security: rebuilt the frontend and API images on PHP 8.5.12 for
      CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619 (unauthenticated RCE, fixed in
      8.5.12). Base images are now pinned to php:8.5-apache instead of a
      floating php:apache, so patch releases still come in but a major
      bump can't land by surprise. Builds now always re-pull the base
      image. (PR #36, commit 20380db)
    - CD can now be run manually ("Run workflow"). A manual run rebuilds
      both images from scratch, without cache, and deploys from main.
      (PRs #35 and #36, commits 1880c1f and 20380db)
2026-10-27:
    - API root (/api/v1/) now returns a machine-readable JSON discovery
      document instead of a plain-text listing: API name and version,
      links to the prose overview, Swagger UI, OpenAPI document, health
      check and CHANGELOG, and the full endpoint list (method, path,
      absolute URL, summary, legacy/deprecated flags). The endpoint list
      is generated from openapi.json at request time, so it can't drift
      from the documented contract. Uses the standard JSON envelope
      (data, CORS, X-AMSAT-API-* headers). Content-Type changes from
      text/plain to application/json; no known consumer parses the old
      text output. (Issue #38, PR #39, commit 9701584)
    - Added an /api front door (api/index.php): a machine-readable JSON
      index of API versions (currently just v1, status "current") with
      each version's base URL, Swagger UI and OpenAPI links. Previously
      /api returned 403. It is deliberately not an alias for the latest
      version -- it points at versioned base URLs and never serves data
      endpoints, so clients pinned to /api/v1/ are never moved silently.
      To ship it in the API image, the build context moved from ./api/v1
      to ./api (Dockerfile now api/Dockerfile, explicit COPYs so build
      files are no longer in the web root; cd.yml contexts and path
      filter, and docker-compose.yml, updated to match). (Issue #38,
      PR #40)
    - CHANGELOG convention (docs/agents/changelog-convention.md): entries
      now reference the PR number, which is required, instead of a
      commit SHA. Squash merges create the final SHA only at merge time,
      so it can't be recorded when the entry is written, and a branch
      SHA doesn't end up on main. Every PR gets an entry unless
      explicitly told otherwise. (PR #41)
2026-10-29:
    - The project now has a license: source code is Apache-2.0 (new
      LICENSE file; composer.json / package.json no longer say
      "proprietary" / "UNLICENSED"), and report data served by the API
      is CC BY 4.0 with attribution. Added API usage guidelines to the
      overview page (overview.php#usage): identify clients with a
      descriptive User-Agent, poll no more than every 5 minutes, submit
      only real observations by the operator whose callsign the report
      carries (apps submitting on their users' behalf are welcome; imports
      from other networks or automated reports need agreement first),
      don't use report data to track operators, and how breaking changes
      are announced. The OpenAPI
      document now carries license, contact and termsOfService. README
      and this file's header point at status.amsat.org. (Issue #42,
      PR #43)
2026-11-01:
    - API usage guidelines (overview.php): caching responses on your own
      infrastructure is expected despite the current Cache-Control:
      no-store header; where to show the data attribution (web footer, or
      an app's credits/about screen); since is for clients keeping their
      own copy of reports, not rolling-window clients. Clarifications from
      API consumer feedback in #47. (PR #48)
2026-11-04:
    - Report timestamp precision migration. BREAKING CHANGE for
      API consumers of GET/POST /api/v1/reports.php -- no deprecation
      window. If you consume this API, read this entry before upgrading:
        - API version is now 1.1 (was 1.0.0), shown in the
          X-AMSAT-API-Version header, the /api/v1/ discovery document,
          health.php and the OpenAPI document. Versions are now
          MAJOR.MINOR: MAJOR matches the URL (/api/v1), MINOR bumps for
          additive changes. This is the last breaking change shipped
          within v1; future breaking changes get a new /api/vN URL.
          API_VERSION in api/v1/lib/version.php is the single source of
          truth, and CI checks the OpenAPI version matches it (Issue #32).
        - `satellite` gains two real columns: `observation_time` (when the
          activity happened -- new source of truth) and `submission_time`
          (when the server received the report; DB-generated only, never
          accepted from request payloads). The old `day`/`hour`/`period`
          columns are frozen as a historical archive -- nothing reads or
          writes them going forward. These are database columns only:
          API field names are unchanged (`reported_at` in,
          `reported_time` out). Column names follow the `_time` suffix
          convention of Google's API design guide (AIP-142).
        - Fixed a real display bug: `reported_time` in GET/POST responses
          previously always showed `:30:00` regardless of when the report
          actually happened (a `CONCAT(day, "T", LPAD(hour,2,"0"),
          ":30:00Z")` reconstruction bug). It now reflects the real
          submitted time, down to the minute.
        - `period` is removed from the GET /reports.php response. It can
          no longer reflect real data going forward.
        - `replaced_count` (never documented) is removed from the POST
          /reports.php response and replaced by `correction`, a boolean:
          true if this report corrects an earlier one from the same
          callsign for the same satellite and 15-minute period (what
          `replaced_count > 0` meant in 1.0), false otherwise. Clients can
          use it to confirm a correction landed in the same period as the
          original. Named per Google AIP-140 (booleans omit an "is"
          prefix), like the existing `legacy` / `deprecated` flags. The
          201 response now has a documented schema in the OpenAPI document.
          The legacy submit form uses it to show "This report corrects the
          previous one", as 1.0 did when it replaced a report.
          The API overview page's POST note, which said nothing is ever
          replaced, now describes corrections.
          Writes are now non-destructive: submitting another report for
          the same satellite/callsign/time no longer deletes the previous
          one -- every submission is stored. What the API returns is
          unchanged from 1.0: a resubmission from the same callsign for the
          same satellite and 15-minute UTC period still corrects the
          earlier report, so GET /reports.php (and legacy sat_info.php)
          return only the latest one and GET /summary.php counts only the
          latest one, exactly as after 1.0's delete+insert. Filters such as
          `status` apply after corrections, so they never return a
          corrected report. The 15-minute periods are :00-:14, :15-:29,
          :30-:44 and :45-:59; 1.0's form used :00-:15, :16-:30, ..., so a
          report entered at exactly :15, :30 or :45 falls in the next
          period. Corrected reports remain in the database; a way to fetch
          them through the API may be added later as an opt-in. The
          `report_count` in GET /catalog.php's optional statistics is a
          raw count of stored reports, corrected ones included.
        - The `since` filter on GET /reports.php now does a real timestamp
          comparison instead of comparing against the buggy reconstructed
          string above -- boundary behavior may differ slightly from
          before for requests near an exact `:30:00`.
        - Backfilled historical data (everything reported before this
          migration shipped) is only accurate to 15-minute-bucket
          precision -- that's the real precision that existed at capture
          time (the old `period` field), not minute-level. Only reports
          submitted after this shipped have true minute precision. 53 of
          712,980 rows in production had no `period` captured at all (a
          legacy sentinel value) and were backfilled to hour-level
          precision instead of 15-minute precision.
      `frontend/v1/submit.php` now POSTs to the API instead of writing to
      MySQL directly (its first use of the site's own API), and its "you
      already made a report" duplicate-replace warning is gone along with
      the behavior it described. `frontend/v1/index.php`'s status grid,
      tooltips, and "Reports From" list were rewritten against
      `observation_time` in the same PR, since they'd otherwise show nothing
      for any report submitted after this shipped.
      Because resubmitting no longer deletes the earlier report, the
      status grid applies the same rule as the API: within each cell
      (unchanged 2-hour columns), only each callsign's latest submission
      per satellite per 15-minute period is counted -- what 1.0's
      delete+insert left behind, so the grid's colours behave exactly as
      before. Corrected reports are still listed in the cell's tooltip,
      greyed out and marked "corrected by a later report". The "To correct
      a report made in error" help text is unchanged and still accurate.
      New tests: tests/StatusGridTest.php, plus API tests for corrections
      in /reports.php, /summary.php and sat_info.php.
      The production DB migration (db/migrations/) is run manually by
      the project owner against the azure193 database container, as part
      of the dallas190 -> status.amsat.org cutover (dump dallas190, load
      it on azure193, migrate, then merge). It is separate from this
      app-code deploy and not wired into CI/CD.
      The migration also adds an index on observation_time alone, for
      time-window queries that don't filter by satellite. A new CI job
      (migration-dry-run) runs the whole migration against a snapshot
      of the pre-migration schema on MariaDB 12.3 and checks the result
      matches db/schema.sql. It caught a bug before production: adding
      submission_time with a CURRENT_TIMESTAMP default would have
      stamped every historical report with the migration's run time.
      Added a CI check (tools/check-timezone-pins.php) that fails the
      build if a new raw DB connection is added without the UTC
      time_zone pin every connection needs for TIMESTAMP columns to
      behave correctly -- a regression guard, not a re-verification of
      today's connections (see the script's header comment and the PR
      description for what it does and doesn't catch).
      The dockerized dev/CD database containers (docker-compose.yml and
      deploy/docker-compose.yml, both mariadb:12.3) now also set
      `--default-time-zone=+00:00` at the server level, as a second
      layer of defense alongside the existing per-connection pinning --
      so a connection would still get UTC even if it somehow lacked the
      explicit `SET time_zone` pin. That now includes production, which
      runs in the azure193 container from this cutover on. The legacy
      dallas190 host, kept only as a rollback fallback, is not covered.
      (Issue #24, PR #30)
    - Frontend and API containers now use PHP's production settings
      (php.ini-production). The image ships no php.ini, so legacy
      warnings were shown to visitors on the submit page. Found during
      the cutover smoke test. (PR #51; entry corrected in PR #54: it
      originally said the form's "remember my callsign" cookie had
      broken, but nothing has read that cookie since 2017.)
    - health.php reports data freshness (newest report's time and age)
      and returns status "degraded" with a reason (no_reports or
      stale_data) when there are no reports or the newest is more than a
      day old. Still HTTP 200 when the database is reachable, 503 only
      when it isn't. API version 1.2 (additive fields). Raised by an API
      consumer: the old SELECT 1 check reported ok on an instance with no
      data. (Issue #47, PR #49)
2026-11-06:
    - submit.php: declared CallSignParser's $callSign property (PHP 8.2+
      deprecation; expected to be an error in PHP 9) and removed a dead
      setcookie("amsatCallsign") call. Nothing has read that cookie since
      2017, and it raised a "headers already sent" warning on every
      submission. (Issue #53, PR #54)
2026-11-07:
    - OpenAPI document and API overview now describe everything the API
      returns: response schemas for summary.php, statuses.php and the
      legacy satellites.php / sat_info.php arrays; the meta and links
      envelope; Satellite links and include_stats fields; and the error
      envelope on every 4xx/5xx response, with each error code listed.
      Also documents existing behavior that wasn't written down: hours
      and limit are clamped to their range rather than rejected, and
      POST /reports.php accepts form data, the satellite/status aliases,
      report value aliases, and legacy year/month/day/hour/minute fields
      (minute defaults to 30 if omitted). No behavior change. A new test
      fails if a response returns a field the spec doesn't document, and
      CI now fails if the committed openapi.json differs from what
      composer openapi generates. (Issue #42, PR #57)
    - The submit form's "Time Heard (UTC)" field is a 24-hour HH:MM text
      field again. PR #30 had replaced the hour/period dropdowns with
      <input type="time">, which browsers render in the device locale,
      so users on en-US devices saw an AM/PM picker next to a UTC label.
      Minute precision from #24 is unchanged. A single-digit hour (9:05)
      is accepted and padded to HH:MM (09:05) by the server-side check
      before use. (PR #55)
    - The #24 migration script now removes rows dated before 1970-01-02
      first (new step 0), matching what was done by hand during the
      cutover after the first run failed on 14 rows dated 1970-01-01
      (below the TIMESTAMP minimum). The CI migration dry run now includes
      such a row. Added an as-run record of the cutover under
      docs/runbooks/. CHANGELOG convention: entries are dated by the date
      the PR merges. (PR #52)
    - GET /reports.php?name= now accepts a satellite's display name as
      well as its API name, as catalog.php already did. A display name
      used to pass the name check and then return 200 with no reports.
      POST /reports.php had the same gap on the write side: a report
      submitted with a display name was stored under that name, so it
      never appeared on the status grid or in GET /reports.php. Both
      now resolve the name to the API name first. No response shape
      change, so no version bump. (Issue #58, PR #60)
    - catalog.php?include_stats=true: report_count now counts reports the
      same way as reports.php and summary.php -- one per callsign per
      satellite per 15-minute period, the latest -- so corrected reports
      no longer inflate it. In 1.0 a correction deleted the earlier
      report, so this restores 1.0's count. It is a bug fix with no
      response-shape change, so no version bump. latest_reported_time is
      unchanged. (Issue #59, PR #61)
    - Status page: the 6-day window in the grid, tooltip and "Reports
      From" queries is now a plain range on observation_time instead of
      a per-row expression, so MariaDB can use
      idx_satellite_observation_time rather than scanning the whole
      satellite table three times per page load. Selects exactly the
      same rows; a new test checks both filters against rows on every
      day boundary. No visible change. (Issue #46, PR #62)
2026-11-08:
    - API overview (polling guidelines): removed the note that
      Cache-Control: no-store "will be relaxed in a future release".
      Issue #56 was closed as not needed at current load, so the page now
      just states the current header. Keeping your own copy of the data
      and serving users from it is still recommended. Docs only.
      (Issue #56, PR #63)
    - API version 1.3. Timestamp fields now match the database column
      names (additive; nothing removed):
      - POST /reports.php accepts observation_time: ISO 8601 with an
        explicit UTC offset (Z, +HH:MM, +HHMM or +HH; a timestamp without an
        offset is rejected, since the time zone can't be guessed).
        reported_at and the
        year/month/day/hour/minute fields still work as deprecated
        aliases with their old lenient parsing. Sending observation_time
        and reported_at with different values returns 422
        conflicting_observation_time. New error codes:
        invalid_observation_time, future_observation_time.
      - /reports.php responses (GET and POST) add observation_time and
        submission_time (when the server received the report; null for
        reports stored before November 2026). catalog.php and summary.php
        add latest_observation_time. reported_time and
        latest_reported_time stay as deprecated aliases with the same
        values. sat_info.php and satellites.php are unchanged.
      - Every JSON envelope response has meta.api_version (same value as
        the X-AMSAT-API-Version header), closing out Issue #32.
      - Deprecated fields stay for the life of /api/v1; the overview page
        has new Versioning and Deprecated fields sections. Requests using
        deprecated input fields are logged with their User-Agent, so we
        can see who still uses them.
      - The v1 submit form now sends observation_time.
      (Issue #45, Issue #32, PR #64)
2026-11-11:
    - CI runs every PHP job on the PHP version the deploy images use, read
      from the FROM lines in api/Dockerfile and frontend/v1/Dockerfile
      (currently 8.5). Before, PHPUnit and the lint jobs used the latest
      PHP image and Playwright used Ubuntu's PHP 8.3. A PHP upgrade is now
      just the two FROM lines, and CI tests the new version with it. CI
      only; no app change. (PR #66)
    - API version 1.4. NORAD catalog numbers (Issue #25):
      - catalog.php and reports.php return norad_cat_id: the satellite's
        NORAD catalog number (the GP/OMM NORAD_CAT_ID field), shared by all
        modes of a spacecraft. null until the spacecraft appears in the
        AMSAT GP feed with an ID.
      - IDs come only from the AMSAT GP feed, via gpdata-sync. The feed
        address is required config with no default in the code: the
        GPDATA_FEED_URL repository variable, written to the server's .env
        by CD. A new CI job fails if it's empty, not https, or doesn't
        serve a valid feed; a run without it aborts and records why. The
        same job fails if the GPDATA_SYNC_TOKEN secret is missing or
        shorter than 32 characters (the only secret passed to CI). Names are matched ignoring
        mode suffixes, dashes, leading zeros and case, and either side of a
        bracketed alternate name. A spacecraft not in the feed keeps its
        ID; an unusable feed aborts the run with no writes; ambiguous
        names are skipped.
      - New POST /api/v1/gpdata-sync.php runs a sync on demand. Admin only:
        requires GPDATA_SYNC_TOKEN as a Bearer token. Documented in
        OpenAPI (Admin tag) and on the overview page.
      - A daily sync (06:17 UTC) runs inside the API container via a
        pinned supercronic scheduler, as www-data. Every run is recorded in
        the new gpdata_sync_run table.
      - Admin dashboard: last run, and a "Sync now" button showing what
        changed and what isn't in the feed. Admin pages show NORAD IDs
        read-only.
      - Schema: satellite_name.norad_cat_id and gpdata_sync_run
        (db/migrations/2026_11_11_*; run before deploying). CI's
        migration dry run now applies every migration in order.
      - submit.php reads the API response status with
        http_get_last_response_headers() instead of $http_response_header,
        which is deprecated in PHP 8.5 and removed in PHP 9. The new
        gpdata-sync code uses the same call.
      (Issue #25, PR #65)
2026-11-12:
    - Decision record docs/agents/api-v1-v2-dividing-line.md: api-v2 will
      be TypeScript; v1 gets only what frontend-v1's public pages need to
      stop querying the database, plus bug and security fixes. Everything
      new goes to api-v2, including the admin pages, which will be
      retired from frontend-v1 once frontend-v2's admin pages ship. #67
      uses cursor paging in reports.php for the grid's full window.
      (PR #70)
    - GET /api/v1/reports.php pages large results: responses carry
      meta.next_cursor and links.next (null on the last page), and a new
      cursor parameter fetches the next page. Paging is keyset-based and
      pinned to a snapshot taken on page 1, so reports arriving mid-paging
      can't cause skipped or repeated rows. A malformed cursor returns
      400 invalid_cursor. This lets the v1 status grid read its full 6-day
      window through the API. API version 1.5.
      (Issue #67, PR #71)
